"Minnesota Supreme Court Opinions (https://mncourts.gov/supremecourt/recentopinions)\nciteturn14view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://mncourts.gov/supremecourt/recentopinions\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 89\nL0: cite0†ADA Accommodation L1: \nL2: Other Languages\nL3: \nL4: cite1†Español cite2†Hmoob cite3†Soomaali cite4†Other L5: \nL6: Menu\nL7: \nL8:   * cite5†Help Topics L9:   * cite6†Find Courts L10:   * cite7†Pay Fines L11:   * cite8†Forms & Instructions L12:   * cite9†Access Case Records L13:   * cite10†File a Case L14: \nL15:   * cite0†ADA Accommodation L16: \nL17: # Minnesota Supreme Court Opinions\nL18: \nL19: ## Overview\nL20: ###\nL21: IMPORTANT NOTICES\nL22: \nL23: Electronic Notification Procedures\nL24: Appellate courts transmit all notices, orders, opinions, and case-related correspondence electronically. To ensure timely receipt of court communications, all attorneys with pending cases must maintain a registered email address with the court.\nL25: Unrepresented parties are also encouraged to participate in the e-notification system by registering their email address. For detailed registration instructions, please visit the cite11†Clerk of Appellate Courts page .\nL26: \nL27: Formatting Update\nL28: The Minnesota Supreme Court has updated the formatting of its published legal opinions and orders. These changes—including standardized alignment and optimized spacing—are part of our commitment to digital accessibility and ensuring our public documents are fully accessible to all individuals.\nL29: ### PREVIOUSLY PUBLISHED OPINIONS\nL30: \nL31: Please visit the cite12†Minnesota State Law Library's Appellate Opinions Archive†mn.gov for previously published Supreme Court Opinions.\nL32: \nL33: About the Courts\nL34: \nL35:   * cite13†About the Courts L36:   * cite14†Careers L37:   * cite15†Court Holidays L38:   * cite16†Publications and Reports L39: \nL40:   * cite17†Minnesota Judicial Council L41:   * cite18†Judicial Officer Directory L42:   * cite19†State Court Administrator's Office L43: \nL44: Helpful Links\nL45:   * cite20†Court Fees L46:   * cite21†Court Rules L47:   * cite22†Self-Help Centers L48:   * cite23†Law Libraries L49: \nL50:   * cite24†Language Access Plan L51:   * cite25†Brochures L52:   * cite26†Archived Documents L53: \nL54: Get Connected\nL55: \nL56:   * cite27†Contact Us L57:   * cite28†Email Updates†service.govdelivery.com L58:   * cite29†News & Public Notices L59:   * cite30†Request a Speaker L60: \nL61:   * cite31†Disclaimer L62:   * cite32†Privacy Policy L63:   * cite33†Terms of Use L64:   * cite34†Sitemap L65: \nL66: To Top Need help? Sign up for updates\nL67: ## Need Help?\nL68: \nL69: Self-Help Centers\nL70: \nL71: Self-Help Centers can help you find helpful information, services, and resources about your legal problem if you are not represented by a lawyer.\nL72: \nL73: cite35†Self-Help Centers L74: \nL75: cite36†Conciliation Court Virtual Assistant L76: \nL77: ## Get Legal Help\nL78: \nL79: cite37†Find a Lawyer L80: \nL81: ## State Law Library\nL82: \nL83: Room G25\nL84: Minnesota Judicial Center\nL85: 25 Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. Blvd.\nL86: St. Paul, MN 55155\nL87: \nL88: cite38†mn.gov/law-library†mn.gov --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n26A305 Postal Service v. California (09/14/2026) (https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/25pdf/26a305_4g15.pdf)\nciteturn14view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 9; Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view0\",\"id\":73}); Total lines: 239\nL0@P0: _________________\nL1@P0: _________________\nL2@P0:  Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026) 1\nL3@P0: KAVANAUGH, J., concurring\nL4@P0: SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\nL5@P0: No. 26A305\nL6@P0: UNITED STATES POSTAL SERVICE, ET AL. v.\nL7@P0: CALIFORNIA, ET AL.\nL8@P0: ON APPLICATION FOR STAY\nL9@P0: [September 14, 2026]\nL10@P0: The application for stay presented to JUSTICE JACKSON\nL11@P0: and by her referred to the Court is denied. The Government\nL12@P0: is unlikely to succeed on the merits of its challenge to the\nL13@P0: District Court’s preliminary injunction. And the equitable\nL14@P0: factors applicable for obtaining emergency relief from this\nL15@P0: Court do not favor a stay.\nL16@P0: JUSTICE KAVANAUGH, concurring.\nL17@P0: In my view, based on the briefing the Court has received\nL18@P0: at this interim stage, there is at least a fair prospect that\nL19@P0: the final rule falls within the Postal Service’s statutory\nL20@P0: authority. See 39 U. S. C. §401(2). But applying the rule\nL21@P0: in the 2026 elections would be arbitrary and capricious in\nL22@P0: violation of the Administrative Procedure Act because state\nL23@P0: and local election officials do not have sufficient time to\nL24@P0: reasonably implement the rule before the elections. See 39\nL25@P0: U. S. C. §3001(m); 5 U. S. C. §706(2)(A). For that reason, I\nL26@P0-1: concur in the denial of the application for stay. _________________\nL27@P1: _________________\nL28@P1:  Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026) 1\nL29@P1: ALITO, J., dissenting\nL30@P1: SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\nL31@P1: No. 26A305\nL32@P1: UNITED STATES POSTAL SERVICE, ET AL. v.\nL33@P1: CALIFORNIA, ET AL.\nL34@P1: ON APPLICATION FOR STAY\nL35@P1: [September 14, 2026]\nL36@P1:  JUSTICE ALITO, with whom JUSTICE THOMAS joins, dis\u0002senting from the denial of application for stay.\nL37@P1: The Court incorrectly denies the Government’s applica\u0002tion for a stay of a universal injunction that bars implemen\u0002tation of a final rule concerning election ballot mail. Some\nL38@P1: of the plaintiffs lack standing, while the remainder are pur\u0002suing a claim that this Court has recently called a “‘Hail\nL39@P1: Mary pass’” that “‘rarely succeeds.’” NRC v. Texas, 605\nL40@P1: U. S. 665, 681–682 (2025). The Government has made the\nL41@P1: showings necessary for a stay, so I respectfully dissent.\nL42@P1: I\nL43@P1: Some five months ago, the President issued an “internal\nL44@P1: directive” to “his subordinates mandating,” among other\nL45@P1: things, that the United States Postal Service “‘initiate a\nL46@P1: proposed rulemaking’” regarding election ballot mail.\nL47@P1: Trump v. California, 609 U. S. ___, ___ (2026) (per curiam)\nL48@P1: (slip op., at 2). Just days after the President’s directive is\u0002sued, numerous organizations and a group of States led by\nL49@P1: California sued in the District of Massachusetts. The Dis\u0002trict Court entered a series of injunctions blocking imple\u0002mentation of the directive. This Court eventually stayed\nL50@P1: one of the injunctions because the District Court likely\nL51@P1: lacked jurisdiction because the suit was premature. Id., at\nL52@P1-2: ___, ___–___ (slip op., at 1, 3–8).\nL53@P2: 2 POSTAL SERVICE v. CALIFORNIA\nL54@P2: ALITO, J., dissenting\nL55@P2: In late August, the Postal Service promulgated a final\nL56@P2: rule pursuant to the President’s directive. Ballot Mail for\nL57@P2: Federal Elections, 91 Fed. Reg. 54966 (2026). The rule re\u0002quires federal ballot mail to be sent in envelopes with three\nL58@P2: special features: an election-mail logo, the ability to be\nL59@P2: scanned using high-speed processing equipment, and a spe\u0002cialized barcode unique to each voter. Id., at 54990–54991.\nL60@P2: States must design envelopes that adhere to these require\u0002ments and submit the designs to the Postal Service for re\u0002view. Ibid. Finally, States must upload basic information\nL61@P2: about the voter to a Postal Service portal. Id., at 54991.\nL62@P2: Under the rule, the Postal Service must review mail sent\nL63@P2: from States to voters for compliance with these require\u0002ments. Ibid. Mailings that fail to comply “will not be ac\u0002cepted and will be returned to the” State, which can then\nL64@P2: resubmit the mail after correcting errors. Ibid.\nL65@P2: Within days of the final rule’s issuance, the District Court\nL66@P2: entered a temporary restraining order that it later con\u0002verted to a nationwide preliminary injunction stopping the\nL67@P2: rule from taking effect. The Government applied to this\nL68@P2: Court for a stay.\nL69@P2: II\nL70@P2: To obtain a stay, the applicant must make a strong show\u0002ing of a likelihood of success on the merits and irreparable\nL71@P2: harm. In close cases, we also consider the balance of the\nL72@P2: equities. See Hollingsworth v. Perry, 558 U. S. 183, 190\nL73@P2: (2010) (per curiam).\nL74@P2: A\nL75@P2: The Government is likely to succeed on the merits of its\nL76@P2: appeal.\nL77@P2: To begin, the District Court issued a nationwide injunc\u0002tion based on claims asserted by several plaintiff organiza\u0002tions that likely lack standing. These organizations first\nL78@P2-3: claim a direct organizational injury because the rule\nL79@P3:  Cite as: 609 U. S. ____ (2026) 3\nL80@P3: ALITO, J., dissenting\nL81@P3: supposedly thwarts their ability to educate members about\nL82@P3: voting. Yet this theory of injury is based on “‘a setback to\nL83@P3: the organization’s abstract social interests’” and an effort\nL84@P3: to “spend its way into standing simply by expending money\nL85@P3: to gather information and advocate against the defendant’s\nL86@P3: action[s].” FDA v. Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, 602\nL87@P3: U. S. 367, 394 (2024). The organizations also assert associ\u0002ational standing based on injuries to unnamed members\nL88@P3: who never filed declarations themselves. Such cryptic fil\u0002ings cannot be used to show standing. We have “required\nL89@P3: plaintiff-organizations to make specific allegations estab\u0002lishing that at least one identified member had suffered or\nL90@P3: would suffer harm” and to additionally meet the “require\u0002ment of naming the affected members.” Summers v. Earth\nL91@P3: Island Institute, 555 U. S. 488, 498–499 (2009).\nL92@P3: As for the plaintiff States, their claims are unlikely to\nL93@P3: succeed. They claim the rule violates the Elections Clause.\nL94@P3: U. S. Const., Art. I, §4, cl. 1. This claim, however, collapses\nL95@P3: into an inquiry about whether the Postal Service has stat\u0002utory authority to promulgate the rule. Even assuming the\nL96@P3: rule here is a regulation of elections, Congress may create\nL97@P3: such rules under the Elections Clause. And, by statute, it\nL98@P3: may authorize the Postal Service to issue such rules. Be\u0002cause litigants cannot convert claims that a federal entity\nL99@P3: has “acted in excess of [its] statutory authority” into consti\u0002tutional claims, see Dalton v. Specter, 511 U. S. 462, 472–\nL100@P3: 474 (1994), the crux of the dispute is whether the Postal\nL101@P3: Service has statutory authority to issue the rule.\nL102@P3: To assert a claim that an agency exceeded its statutory\nL103@P3: authority, the challenger needs a cause of action, which is\nL104@P3: typically provided by the Administrative Procedure Act.\nL105@P3: 5 U. S. C. §§702, 704. However, Congress broadly ex\u0002empted “the exercise of the powers of the Postal Service”\nL106@P3: from review under the APA. 39 U. S. C. §410(a); see also\nL107@P3: Mittleman v. Postal Regulatory Comm’n, 757 F. 3d 300, 305\nL108@P3-4: (CADC 2014). It also channeled many claims against the\nL109@P4: 4 POSTAL SERVICE v. CALIFORNIA\nL110@P4: ALITO, J., dissenting\nL111@P4: Postal Service to initial review before the Postal Regulatory\nL112@P4: Commission followed by review in the United States Court\nL113@P4: of Appeals for the District of Columbia. See 39 U. S. C.\nL114@P4: §§3662–3663. These provisions tightly constrain APA re\u0002view of Postal Service actions.\nL115@P4: Unsurprisingly, the statutory claim here is not subject to\nL116@P4: APA review. It is a challenge to the scope of the Postal Ser\u0002vice’s general rulemaking authority and therefore exempt\nL117@P4: from the APA. §410(a).*\nL118@P4: Since the plaintiff States cannot proceed under the APA,\nL119@P4: they must instead invoke an equitable cause of action\nL120@P4: known as an ultra vires claim. Because such claims rely on\nL121@P4: a judicially-implied cause of action and may be used to “end\u0002run” statutes created by Congress that restrict judicial re\u0002view, this Court’s “cases have strictly limited nonstatutory\nL122@P4: ultra vires review” to a narrow set of circumstances. NRC\nL123@P4: v. Texas, 605 U. S., at 681. Crucially, the relevant agency\nL124@P4: action must be “entirely in ‘excess of [the agency’s] dele\u0002gated powers and contrary to a specific prohibition’ in a\nL125@P4: statute.” Ibid. (emphasis deleted). Such a claim is thus\nL126@P4: “‘essentially a Hail Mary pass’” that “‘rarely succeeds.’”\nL127@P4: Id., at 681–682; see also Federal Express Corp. v. Depart\u0002ment of Commerce, 39 F. 4th 756, 764 (CADC 2022) (“ultra\nL128@P4: vires claims are confined to ‘extreme’ agency error where\nL129@P4: the agency has ‘stepped so plainly beyond the bounds of [its\nL130@P4: statutory authority], or acted so clearly in defiance of it, as\nL131@P4: to warrant the immediate intervention of an equity court’”).\nL132@P4: That Hail Mary pass is unlikely to be successfully com\u0002pleted here. The Postal Service has broad authority to\nL133@P4: ——————\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn14view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view0\",\"id\":75}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn14view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: click({\"ref_id\":\"turn13view0\",\"id\":76}); Total lines: 1\n"
